## Z — EXAM PATTERN: walk up a hierarchy tree, count all descendants

store all rows in arrays first (two-pass approach)
for each employee, walk up the tree and increment each ancestor's count
print in numeric order (awk arrays are unordered, so track maxid)

| Construct | Meaning |
|-----------|----------|
| `BEGIN { FS="," }` | set comma separator before reading input |
| `id[$1]=$1` | store fields in arrays keyed by employee id |
| `while (p != 0)` | walk up: p = manager of p, stop at root (0) |
| `subs[p]++` | each ancestor gets +1 for this employee |
| `for (i=1;i<=maxid)` | print in order (arrays are unordered in awk) |
| `(subs[i]+0)` | if unset, +0 forces it to 0 instead of "" |

---

## X — LINE BY LINE

```awk
# █ FILLED BY STUDENT: 
BEGIN { FS=","; maxid=0 }
{
    id[$1] = $1; name[$1] = $2; manager[$1] = $3
    if ($1+0 > maxid) maxid = $1+0
}
END {
    for (emp in id) {
        p = manager[emp]+0
        while (p != 0) { subs[p]++; p = manager[p]+0 }
    }
    for (i = 1; i <= maxid; i++)
        if (i in name) print i "," name[i] "," (subs[i]+0)
}
```

- BEGIN { FS="," }      → set comma as field separator before any input is read
- id[$1]=$1             → store each employee id (used later to check existence)
- manager[$1]=$3        → who does each employee report to
- $1+0                  → force string → number conversion
- maxid                 → track highest ID to print results in order 1..maxid
- p = manager[emp]+0    → start at this employee's direct manager
- while (p != 0)        → 0 = "no manager" (root of tree); stop there
- subs[p]++             → this ancestor has one more descendant
- p = manager[p]+0      → move one level up the tree
- (subs[i]+0)           → unset array element = "" in awk; +0 converts to 0

