## X — LINE BY LINE

```awk
# █ FILLED BY STUDENT: 
BEGIN { FS=":" }
{
    user = $1
    caret = index($2, "^")                    # position of ^ in "process^cpu"
    proc = substr($2, 1, caret - 1)           # process name: before ^
    cpu  = substr($2, caret + 1) + 0          # cpu value: after ^, cast to number
    key  = user SUBSEP proc                    # 2D key: "alice" + separator + "python"
    total[key] += cpu                          # accumulate CPU for this user+process
    if (!(user in seen)) { order[++n_users] = user; seen[user] = 1 }
}
END {
    for (i = 1; i <= n_users; i++) {
        u = order[i]
        max_cpu = -1; best = ""
        for (key in total) {
            split(key, k, SUBSEP)              # k[1]=user, k[2]=process
            if (k[1] == u && total[key] > max_cpu) {
                max_cpu = total[key]; best = k[2]
            }
        }
        print u "," best
    }
}
```

- index($2, "^")         → returns 1-based position of ^ in $2 (0 if absent)
- substr(s, start, len)  → start is 1-based; len is optional (goes to end if omitted)
- SUBSEP                 → built-in awk multi-key separator (ASCII 034, non-printable)
- !(user in seen)        → "in" tests array membership; ! = NOT; use to track first-seen
- order[++n_users]       → pre-increment: put user in next slot to preserve input order
- split(key, k, SUBSEP)  → splits key back: k[1]=user, k[2]=process
- total[key] += cpu      → awk auto-initialises numeric arrays to 0

